const FN84 = `Can You Spot Fake News?\nEvery day we watch, hear, or read interesting news. However, have you ever seriously considered whether an article is really true? Everyone likes an interesting news story but (A)________ it is completely made up?\nFake news can be very harmful (B)________ it can make people less informed or even misled. (C)________, there have been various fake news reports throughout history. Why have some people written such false information? Let's look into some articles thinking about the hidden motives behind them.`;
add(R, passage(P, FN84, `위 글의 빈칸 (A), (B), (C)에 들어갈 말로 알맞게 연결된 것은?`), '1', ['(A) what if – (B) in that – (C) Nevertheless', '(A) whether – (B) because – (C) Furthermore', '(A) whether – (B) in that – (C) Nevertheless', '(A) what if – (B) because – (C) Furthermore', '(A) what if – (B) that – (C) Nevertheless']);
add(R, passage(P, FN84, `위 글의 내용과 일치하는 것을 고르시오.\n\nWhich is correct according to the text?`), '4', ['It is hard to find fake news in history.', 'The writer will show us some examples of advertisements.', 'Many people seriously consider whether an article is true.', 'Some people write articles with hidden motives behind them.', 'Interesting news is helpful in that it can make us informed.']);
const SLAV90 = `SLAV SHOOTS A FRIEND IN ARGUMENT\nMejk Swenekafew, a Slav worker at the Columbia Coal Mine, was shot and ⓐ<u>seriously wounded</u> by John Pecitello near the mining camp Thursday evening.\nThe two men had an argument ⓑ<u>during</u> a meeting. The argument led to a fight, and Pecitello shot Swenekafew twice, in the chest and leg. He is now at the hospital in critical condition. Pecitello ran away after the shooting. The police are searching for him now and are warning citizens ⓒ<u>who</u> he is extremely dangerous.\n-The Daily Telegram\n\nIs there ⓓ<u>anything strange</u> about the article? Read the Slav's name backwards; it spells "we-fake-news." Who wrote this and why?\nThe Daily Telegram published this fake article so that they could prove if The Daily News, their competitor, was stealing their articles. The Daily News published the same article about "Swenekafew" the next day and thus ⓔ<u>got caught stealing</u>. The people at The Daily News had to admit their act and were harshly criticized by the public.`;
add(R, passage(P, SLAV90, `위 글의 밑줄 친 ⓐ~ⓔ 중, 어법상 어색한 것은?`), '3', CIRCLE);
add(R, passage(P, SLAV90, `위 글을 요약한 표의 내용 중 알맞지 않은 것은?\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| A News Story | It was news (A)<u>about a Slav worker who was shot and injured</u>. (B)<u>Pecitello ran away after the shooting</u>. |\n| Motive | The Daily Telegram wanted to prove that (C)<u>their competitor was stealing their articles</u>. |\n| Result | (D)<u>The people at the Daily News did not admit their act</u> and (E)<u>was criticized by the public</u>. |`), '4', ABCDE);
const SLAV92 = `SLAV SHOOTS A FRIEND IN ARGUMENT\n-The Daily Telegram September 19th, 1903\nMejk Swenekafew, a Slav worker at the Columbia Coal Mine, was shot and seriously wounded by John Pecitello near the mining camp Thursday evening.\nThe two men had an argument during a meeting. The argument led to a fight, and Pecitello shot him twice, in the chest and leg. He is now at the hospital in critical condition. Pecitello ran away after the shooting. The police are searching for him now and are warning citizens that he is extremely dangerous. Fortunately, the man who was shot by his friend saved his life.\n\n${SLAV_B}`;
add(R, passage(P, SLAV92, `위 글을 읽고 대답할 수 없는 것은?`), '3', ['What did Swenekafew do for a living?', 'What did Pecitello do after the shooting?', 'Why did Pecitello have an argument with Swenekafew?', 'Was there any special meaning to Swenekafew?', 'Why was The Daily News harshly criticized by the public?']);
add(R, passage(P, SLAV92, `위 글을 읽고 아래 질문에 대한 답이 되도록 빈칸에 들어갈 알맞은 말을 고르면?\n\nQ: What did The Daily Telegram want to prove by publishing the article, "Slav Shoots a Friend in Argument?"\nA: They wanted to prove that ______________________.`), '5', ['The Daily News reported Slav worker was really in danger and needed a help', 'The Daily News was publishing not only crime articles but also sports articles', 'The Daily News helped to protect human rights of criminals', 'The Daily News reported a wrong police report', 'The Daily News was stealing their articles']);
const AD94 = `Last night, an angry group of rhinoceroses (A)[broke / has broken] down the walls of the cage at the zoo and escaped. They also broke down the walls of the other wild animals' cages. These animals ran down the streets and injured hundreds of people. Twelve of the animals are still on the loose. Citizens should stay indoors until further notice.\n*Not a single act or incident (B)[describing / described] above has taken place.\n\nAt that time, those who read the article carefully laughed out loud. Those who didn't read it to the end got really worried. Not knowing the news was false, many people panicked. Some tried (C)[escaping / to escape] the city while others went into the parks with guns to hunt the animals. So why did The Herald make up such news? Later, they confessed that they made it up so that they could draw the readers' attention to the ⓐ________ conditions at the zoo.`;
add(R, passage(P, AD94, `위 글의 괄호 (A), (B), (C)에서 어법상 알맞은 말로 바르게 연결된 것은?`), '4', ['(A) has broken – (B) described – (C) to escape', '(A) broke – (B) describing – (C) to escape', '(A) broke – (B) described – (C) escaping', '(A) broke – (B) described – (C) to escape', '(A) has broken – (B) describing – (C) escaping']);
add(R, passage(P, AD94, `위 글의 빈칸 ⓐ에 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?`), '5', ['safe', 'reliable', 'secure', 'various', 'uncomfortable']);
add(R, passage(P, AD94, `위 글의 내용을 가장 잘 요약한 것은?`), '4', ['A zoo keeper unlocked the cages of animals.', 'Fake news made people run away from home.', 'Animals were smart enough to unlock the cage.', 'The press used an article to indicate the condition of zoo.', 'The media press deceived people only for their own profit.']);
add(R, passage(P, HOW99, `위 글의 (A)~(D) 중, 주어진 문장이 들어갈 곳으로 알맞은 곳은?\n\nIs it a new or an old story?`), '2', ['(A)', '(B)', '(C)', '(D)', '없다']);
const SLAV135 = `${SLAV}\n\nThe article was a special case, but there are many "fake" news articles published every day. As readers, we need to read critically and judge whether the news is real or fake.`;
add(R, passage(P, SLAV135, `위 글을 아래와 같이 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A)~(C)에 들어갈 말을 본문에서 찾아 한 단어로 각각 쓰시오.\n\n• The Fake News Trap\nIn 1903, The Daily Telegram published the (A)________ article to prove if their competitor (B)________ ________ their articles. The story was about a slav worker named Mejk Swenekafew being shot. The same story (C)________ ________ in The Daily News the next day. Due to the article, The Daily News was caught copying it and had to admit their wrong act.`), 'fake / was stealing / was published', null, { subParts: [{ label: '(A)', answer: 'fake', acceptedAnswers: ['false'] }, { label: '(B)', answer: 'was stealing' }, { label: '(C)', answer: 'was published' }] });
const FNAD151 = `(A) Every day we watch, hear, or read interesting news. However, have you ever seriously considered whether an article is really true? Everyone likes an interesting news story but ⓐ<u>what if is it completely made up</u>?\n(B) Fake news can be very harmful in that it can make ⓑ<u>people less informed or even misled</u>. Nevertheless, there have been various fake news reports throughout history. Why have some people written such false information? Let's look into some articles ⓒ<u>thinking about the hidden motives behind them</u>.\n\nAWFUL DISASTER\n(C) Last night, an angry group of rhinoceroses broke down the walls of the cage at the zoo and escaped. They also broke down the walls of the other wild animals' cages. These animals ran down the streets and injured hundreds of people. Twelve of the animals are still on the loose. Citizens should stay indoors until further notice.\nⓓ<u>Not a single act or incident described above has taken place</u>. - The Herald November 9th, 1874\n(D) At that time, those who read the article carefully laughed out loud. Those who didn't read it to the end got really worried. (A)<u>Because they didn't know the news was false</u>, many people panicked. Some tried to escape the city while others went into the parks with guns to hunt the animals.\nSo why did The Herald make up such news? Later, they confessed that they made it up ⓔ<u>so that they could draw the readers' attention to the unsafe conditions at the zoo</u>.`;
add(R, passage(P, FNAD151, `위 글의 밑줄 친 (A)를 6단어의 분사구문으로 바꾸어 쓰시오.\n\n→ ______________________, many people panicked.`), 'Not knowing the news was false', null);
add(R, `다음 글을 읽고, Anna와 Mike가 나눈 대화의 내용 중 가짜뉴스를 찾아낼 때 고려해야 할 사항과 일치하지 않는 것은?\n\nHow to spot fake news!\n• Consider the Source.\nIs it from a reliable source? Can we trust the writer?\n• Check the Date\nIs it a new or an old story? Is it related to current events?\n• Read Beyond the Headlines\nDoes the headline match the content?\n• Find Supporting Sources.\nDo other related stories provide similar content?\n\nAnna: Mike, do you believe that every published article is true?\nMike: No, I try to read articles critically.\nAnna: Oh, how?\nMike: First, ⓐ<u>I read beyond the headline to see if the headline matches the content</u>. Then, ⓑ<u>I consider the source to check if the story is from a reliable source</u> or ⓒ<u>if the reader can be trusted</u>. How about you?\nAnna: ⓓ<u>I check the date of the article to see if the story is related to current events</u>. I also find supporting sources. In other words, ⓔ<u>I check if other related stories provide similar content</u>.`, '3', CIRCLE);
add(R, passage(P, WING, `위 글을 읽고 아래 질문에 대한 답이 되도록 빈칸에 들어갈 알맞은 말을 고르면?\n\nQ: What are the strong points of the product, new walking shoes?\nA: When people walk with Wing Walker, they ______________________.`), '2', ['use the hidden board technology', 'burn more calories and get slimmer', 'consume less calories and get slim', 'can strength their new walking shoes', 'may not upgrade their walking experience']);

